277
00:19:52,141 --> 00:19:55,272
have some time to look
at questions 26 to 30.

278
00:20:25,109 --> 00:20:29,260
Now listen and answer questions 26 to 30.

279
00:20:30,459 --> 00:20:35,260
Remind me why you chose that stretch
of river. It's not the most obvious site.

280
00:20:35,261 --> 00:20:38,670
Partly access. There's
a footpath right down

281
00:20:38,694 --> 00:20:42,260
to the bank, so carrying
all the kit was easy.

282
00:20:42,261 --> 00:20:45,748
But honestly, if that had
been the only thing, we'd have

283
00:20:45,772 --> 00:20:49,272
gone to the bridge site,
which is even easier to get to.

284
00:20:51,260 --> 00:20:55,299
What actually decided it was that the
department has records from exactly that

285
00:20:55,323 --> 00:20:59,272
stretch going back to the 1990s, so
we could set our results against theirs.

286
00:20:59,273 --> 00:21:05,260
The meanders are lovely and pronounced
as well, but that wasn't really why.

287
00:21:06,759 --> 00:21:10,639
Good. Now, your erosion
pins. You put 12 into the outer

288
00:21:10,663 --> 00:21:14,272
bank in March and measured
them again in September.

289
00:21:14,559 --> 00:21:16,260
We know 12 isn't many.

290
00:21:16,809 --> 00:21:19,864
Actually, I don't think
the number's the issue.

291
00:21:19,888 --> 00:21:23,272
12 is perfectly respectable
for a project this size.

292
00:21:24,109 --> 00:21:27,141
And you mentioned cattle
possibly knocking them,

293
00:21:27,165 --> 00:21:30,272
but you said yourselves
the field was fenced off.

294
00:21:30,859 --> 00:21:34,742
What worries me is that March
to September misses the winter

295
00:21:34,766 --> 00:21:38,260
floods, and that's when
most bank erosion takes place.

296
00:21:38,261 --> 00:21:41,260
Right. We hadn't thought of that.

297
00:21:42,260 --> 00:21:46,130
Don't panic. You can't go back
and measure in January now, but you

298
00:21:46,154 --> 00:21:50,272
must acknowledge it and say what
you'd expect a winter record to show.

299
00:21:50,273 --> 00:21:55,260
Callum, how did you feel when
you saw the outer bank figures?

300
00:21:57,260 --> 00:22:01,452
Honestly, I'd expected the bank to
have retreated by tens of centimeters

301
00:22:01,476 --> 00:22:05,272
from everything in the textbooks,
and it had hardly moved at all.

302
00:22:07,709 --> 00:22:11,614
It's not that I doubted the readings. We
measured every pin twice, and then nothing

303
00:22:11,638 --> 00:22:15,272
like the department's older figures
either, which showed much faster retreat.

304
00:22:15,809 --> 00:22:19,260
So how are you going to explain
that difference from the earlier data?

305
00:22:20,260 --> 00:22:24,495
I think it's the stone wall the landowner
built upstream about 10 years ago.

306
00:22:24,519 --> 00:22:28,272
It's protecting the bank, so
there's less energy reaching our site.

307
00:22:29,260 --> 00:22:32,362
I'm not convinced. The
wall's 300 metres upstream.

308
00:22:32,386 --> 00:22:37,272
I'd say it's simply been a run of drier
years, so there's been lower discharge.

309
00:22:37,273 --> 00:22:41,260
We've been going round
in circles on that for a week.

310
00:22:41,409 --> 00:22:44,516
Well, at least you
agree on everything else.

311
00:22:44,540 --> 00:22:48,272
You're both happy with
scattergraphs for the results?

312
00:22:48,459 --> 00:22:50,260
Aye, no argument there.

313
00:22:50,409 --> 00:22:52,542
And we both want to
keep the bits from our

314
00:22:52,566 --> 00:22:55,272
conversation with the
farmer. That was really useful.

315
00:23:01,509 --> 00:23:05,232
Then present both explanations and weigh them
up. And before you submit, I'd normally suggest

316
00:23:05,256 --> 00:23:09,272
swapping drafts with another group, but nobody else
worked on your stretch, so that won't help much.

317
00:23:12,459 --> 00:23:16,368
Your reference list is fine. Don't touch it.
What I'd like you to do is read Pearson's article

318
00:23:16,392 --> 00:23:20,272
on artificial bank protection. It'll give you
the evidence to judge Hannah's theory properly.

319
00:23:20,273 --> 00:23:26,260
Great. More reading.
No, fair enough. Thanks, Dr. Mercer.

320
00:23:26,261 --> 00:23:28,518
That is the end of part three.

321
00:23:28,542 --> 00:23:33,272
You now have one minute to
check your answers to part three.

322
00:23:50,260 --> 00:23:51,260
Thank you.

323
00:23:51,261 --> 00:23:52,260
Thank you, Dr. Mercer.

324
