325
00:24:34,260 --> 00:24:38,017
Part four. You will hear
a lecturer giving a talk

326
00:24:38,041 --> 00:24:42,272
about the economics of the
medieval English wool trade.

327
00:24:44,260 --> 00:24:48,135
First, you have some time
to look at questions 31 to

328
00:24:48,159 --> 00:24:52,272
40. Now listen carefully and
answer questions 31 to 40.

329
00:24:52,509 --> 00:24:56,547
Good morning. Today we're
turning to what was, for several

330
00:24:56,571 --> 00:25:00,272
centuries, the backbone of
the English economy: wool.

331
00:25:00,459 --> 00:25:03,269
One historian remarked
that England in this period was

332
00:25:03,293 --> 00:25:06,272
little more than a sheep farm
with a government attached.

333
00:25:06,273 --> 00:25:10,260
An exaggeration, certainly,
but not a huge one.

334
00:25:16,259 --> 00:25:20,227
So why was English wool so sought after? It wasn't
a question of volume. Later on, Spain produced

335
00:25:20,251 --> 00:25:24,272
considerably more. Nor was it the colour, which
actually varied a good deal from region to region.

336
00:25:28,259 --> 00:25:32,186
What buyers in Flanders and Italy were paying for
was its fineness, by which I mean how thin and

337
00:25:32,210 --> 00:25:36,272
soft the individual fibres were, because that
determined the quality of the cloth they could weave.

338
00:25:37,260 --> 00:25:40,851
Now, you might assume that
the great flocks belonged to the

339
00:25:40,875 --> 00:25:45,272
aristocracy, to earls and barons
with vast estates. Some did, of course.

340
00:25:49,260 --> 00:25:53,161
But the really large-scale producers were
monasteries, particularly those of the Cistercian

341
00:25:53,185 --> 00:25:57,272
order, some of which kept 10 or 15,000 sheep on
the uplands of Yorkshire and the Welsh borders.

342
00:25:58,260 --> 00:26:02,356
Wool was traded by the sack,
a standard unit of about 364

343
00:26:02,380 --> 00:26:06,272
pounds in weight, or roughly
the fleeces of 250 sheep.

344
00:26:06,859 --> 00:26:10,342
At its peak, in the early
14th century, England was

345
00:26:10,366 --> 00:26:14,272
exporting somewhere in the
region of 35,000 sacks a year.

346
00:26:14,809 --> 00:26:19,260
What's economically fascinating is
how these monasteries sold their wool.

347
00:26:19,609 --> 00:26:23,011
Italian and Flemish merchants
would pay for it in advance,

348
00:26:23,035 --> 00:26:26,272
sometimes for the next 10
or even 20 years' production.

349
00:26:26,909 --> 00:26:29,120
Now, this wasn't strictly
a loan, and it wasn't

350
00:26:29,144 --> 00:26:31,272
really investment in
the modern sense either.

351
00:26:31,273 --> 00:26:35,260
Economic historians generally
describe it as a form of credit.

352
00:26:35,261 --> 00:26:40,260
The abbeys were, in effect, borrowing
against wool that hadn't yet grown.

353
00:26:40,309 --> 00:26:42,260
And this was a risky arrangement.

354
00:26:42,261 --> 00:26:45,688
If an epidemic swept through
a flock, an abbey could find

355
00:26:45,712 --> 00:26:49,272
itself owing years of wool
that it simply couldn't deliver.

356
00:26:49,459 --> 00:26:54,260
The term contemporaries used
for these outbreaks was murrain.

357
00:26:54,409 --> 00:26:57,260
That's M-U-R-R-A-I-N.

358
00:26:57,809 --> 00:27:02,260
It was a rather vague word, covering
almost any disease of cattle or sheep.

359
00:27:02,459 --> 00:27:07,260
And several abbeys were left heavily in
debt by murrain in the late 13th century.

360
00:27:07,459 --> 00:27:10,319
Let's turn now to the
crown, because English kings

361
00:27:10,343 --> 00:27:13,272
were very quick to see
wool as a source of revenue.

362
00:27:13,609 --> 00:27:18,260
Export duties on wool became one
of the largest items of royal income.

363
00:27:18,609 --> 00:27:22,135
It's often said that this
money went into building

364
00:27:22,159 --> 00:27:25,260
castles, but in fact,
remarkably little did.

365
00:27:25,261 --> 00:27:28,260
Overwhelmingly, it paid
for military campaigns.

366
00:27:28,459 --> 00:27:33,260
Above all, Edward III's campaigns in
France during the Hundred Years' War.

367
00:27:33,459 --> 00:27:37,260
To manage all this, the crown
created what was called the Staple.

368
00:27:37,859 --> 00:27:42,260
A single designated town through
which all wool exports had to pass.

369
00:27:42,261 --> 00:27:45,991
The Staple moved around
a great deal at first, between

370
00:27:46,015 --> 00:27:49,272
Bruges, Antwerp, and a
number of English ports.

371
00:27:49,434 --> 00:27:54,260
But from 1363, it was fixed at
Calais, then an English possession.

372
00:27:54,759 --> 00:27:57,260
And it remained there for almost 200 years.

373
00:27:57,261 --> 00:28:00,748
Officially, the Staple existed
to guarantee quality, so

374
00:28:00,772 --> 00:28:04,260
that foreign buyers could
trust what they were getting.

375
00:28:04,261 --> 00:28:06,260
That was the justification.

376
00:28:06,261 --> 00:28:09,260
But the real motive was far more practical.

377
00:28:09,484 --> 00:28:13,360
With all the wool passing
through one place, collection

378
00:28:13,384 --> 00:28:17,260
of the tax became much
simpler and far harder to evade.

379
00:28:17,261 --> 00:28:20,678
The merchants who ran
the Staple became extremely

380
00:28:20,702 --> 00:28:24,260
wealthy, and they regularly
lent money to the king.

381
00:28:24,261 --> 00:28:26,660
Sometimes in return
for reductions in the

382
00:28:26,684 --> 00:28:29,272
very duties they were
supposed to be paying.

383
00:28:29,334 --> 00:28:32,785
So, the relationship
between the crown and the

384
00:28:32,809 --> 00:28:36,272
wool merchants was,
shall we say, rather cosy.

385
00:28:37,109 --> 00:28:41,260
Now, these heavy duties on raw
wool had an important side effect.

386
00:28:41,909 --> 00:28:44,260
Finished cloth was taxed much more lightly.

387
00:28:44,784 --> 00:28:49,260
So, it increasingly made sense to weave the
cloth in England and export that instead.

388
00:28:49,309 --> 00:28:54,260
And this shift was helped along by
a change in a process called fulling.

389
00:28:54,261 --> 00:28:59,260
Fulling means beating woven
cloth in water to thicken and clean it.

390
00:28:59,459 --> 00:29:03,260
And traditionally, it was done by
workers trampling it with their feet.

391
00:29:03,260 --> 00:29:07,260
By the 14th century, fulling
was increasingly mechanized.

392
00:29:07,559 --> 00:29:12,260
Wind power was tried in some flatter
areas, but it proved too unreliable.

393
00:29:13,034 --> 00:29:15,260
The mills that succeeded
were driven by water,

394
00:29:15,261 --> 00:29:19,346
which is why the cloth industry
gradually drifted away from the

395
00:29:19,370 --> 00:29:23,260
old towns towards hilly
districts with fast flowing streams,

396
00:29:23,261 --> 00:29:26,260
such as the Cotswolds
and the Pennine Valleys.

397
00:29:26,309 --> 00:29:29,260
You can still see the
profits of all this today.

398
00:29:29,261 --> 00:29:34,260
Wealthy wool and cloth merchants
spent lavishly on their parish churches.

399
00:29:34,659 --> 00:29:37,790
And the so-called wool
churches of Suffolk and the

400
00:29:37,814 --> 00:29:41,272
Cotswolds are strikingly
large for such small villages.

401
00:29:41,273 --> 00:29:45,260
If you visit, notice that it
isn't spires that dominate.

402
00:29:45,509 --> 00:29:48,260
Spires are actually
quite rare in these areas.

403
00:29:48,309 --> 00:29:51,516
What you see are tall,
elaborately decorated

404
00:29:51,540 --> 00:29:55,272
towers, often paid for by
a single merchant family.

405
00:29:55,959 --> 00:29:58,260
One more factor deserves a mention.

406
00:29:58,509 --> 00:30:04,260
After the Black Death in 1348,
labour became scarce and expensive,

407
00:30:04,359 --> 00:30:08,260
and many landowners turned
their fields over from crops to sheep,

408
00:30:08,359 --> 00:30:10,825
since a single shepherd
could look after land

409
00:30:10,849 --> 00:30:13,272
that had once needed
dozens of farm workers.

410
00:30:13,709 --> 00:30:18,260
That kept wool production high, even
as exports of raw wool began to fall.

411
00:30:18,261 --> 00:30:21,260
Finally, decline.

412
00:30:21,359 --> 00:30:26,260
By the 16th century, English raw wool
no longer dominated European markets.

413
00:30:26,484 --> 00:30:31,260
Partly that was because English weavers
were now using so much of it themselves.

414
00:30:31,509 --> 00:30:34,260
But there was also serious
competition from Spain,

415
00:30:34,709 --> 00:30:38,260
where a breed with an exceptionally
fine fleece had been developed.

416
00:30:38,609 --> 00:30:43,260
Spanish merino wool gradually
took over the luxury end of the market.

417
00:30:43,559 --> 00:30:47,260
The legacy of the trade
survives in some odd places.

418
00:30:47,260 --> 00:30:51,260
The Lord Speaker in the House
of Lords still sits on the wool sack,

419
00:30:51,609 --> 00:30:54,645
a large cushion stuffed
with wool, as a reminder

420
00:30:54,669 --> 00:30:57,272
of where England's
wealth once came from.

421
00:30:57,459 --> 00:31:01,260
Next week, we'll look at how the
cloth industry developed in its own right.

422
00:31:01,261 --> 00:31:04,260
That is the end of the listening test.

